Let the functional harness be pointed at a datastore that is neither
SQL nor Cassandra

Why this patch exists
=====================

The evidence this repository wants for its layer is upstream's own
functional suites — real frontend, history and matching, real workers,
real workflows — running against a custom datastore. Everything that
takes to boot such a cluster is already exported and already threaded:
NewTestClusterFactoryWithCustomTestBaseFactory takes a
PersistenceTestBaseFactory, and newClusterWithPersistenceTestBaseFactory
hands that base's AbstractDataStoreFactory to the server it starts
(tests/testcore/test_cluster.go). The harness was built for exactly this
kind of plugin.

What stands in the way is one line. Every suite in ./tests embeds
FunctionalTestBase, whose SetupSuite builds its cluster from
NewTestClusterFactory() (functional_test_base.go) — the no-argument
constructor, which always builds defaultPersistenceTestBaseFactory.
That factory switches on the -persistenceType / -persistenceDriver
flags and panics on anything but SQL or NoSQL/Cassandra:

	panic(fmt.Sprintf("unknown store type: %v", options.StoreType))

So a suite cannot be handed a factory from outside, whatever it is
composed of: the call site is inside somebody else's SetupSuite, and no
flag value reaches a store the harness does not know. A test harness is
never shipped and the suites are upstream's own, so a patched checkout
is the whole of what this needs — but it does need one.

How it was needed
=================

Measured rather than argued, in a module with
`replace go.temporal.io/server => ./temporal` over a patched checkout.
waltz itself has no such checkout and no replace directive; it builds
against the stock release, and this patch is for the harness only:

  * `go build -tags test_dep go.temporal.io/server/tests/testcore`
    compiles clean with the patch applied. Without `-tags test_dep` the
    package does not compile at all, patched or not — upstream's own
    switch for common/testing/testhooks;
  * a probe package implementing PersistenceTestBaseFactory, assigning
    it to the new variable and calling NewTestClusterFactory() compiles
    against the patched checkout, which is the seam in the shape a live
    harness will use it. Importing
    common/persistence/persistence-tests to build a *TestBase adds five
    indirect requirements to that module's go.mod (go-version,
    json-iterator/go, modern-go/concurrent, modern-go/reflect2,
    collector/pdata); tests/testcore on its own adds none.

Shape of the patch
==================

A package-level variable and three lines at the one call site that
cannot take an argument. It is deliberately not an option on
FunctionalTestBase and not a parameter: what persistence the harness
uses is already process-level configuration in this package — cliFlags,
set from TestMain, read by the same function — and the override sits in
that same slot. Which suite runs does not get to change it, and neither
would a flag.

Hooking NewTestClusterFactory rather than SetupSuite is what keeps the
patch to one file and one function: the other three hardcoded call
sites (tests/ndc, tests/xdc) are out of this repository's scope and get
the same treatment for free, without a line each.

Upstreamability
===============

This is the patch this repository would most like to delete, and the
way to delete it is a PR: the hook one layer down is already exported
and public, so upstreaming this is threading the last inch of a seam
somebody at Temporal already built. Until then it is a patch against a
checkout that nothing shipped compiles against: no package under
go.temporal.io/server/tests is reachable from waltz's own build, which
is why applying it changes nothing this repository ships.

diff --git a/tests/testcore/test_cluster.go b/tests/testcore/test_cluster.go
index c3a1b384c..64ad5a733 100644
--- a/tests/testcore/test_cluster.go
+++ b/tests/testcore/test_cluster.go
@@ -128,8 +128,22 @@ func (f *defaultTestClusterFactory) NewCluster(t *testing.T, clusterConfig *Test
 	return newClusterWithPersistenceTestBaseFactory(t, clusterConfig, logger, f.tbFactory)
 }
 
+// CustomPersistenceTestBaseFactory, when set, replaces the persistence test base
+// NewTestClusterFactory would otherwise build from -persistenceType and
+// -persistenceDriver. It is how a datastore implemented outside this repository —
+// an AbstractDataStoreFactory of its own — is put under the functional suites:
+// they reach the harness only through FunctionalTestBase, which builds its
+// cluster from NewTestClusterFactory and so can never be handed a factory
+// through NewTestClusterFactoryWithCustomTestBaseFactory.
+//
+// Set it from TestMain, before any suite runs. It is read once per cluster.
+var CustomPersistenceTestBaseFactory PersistenceTestBaseFactory
+
 func NewTestClusterFactory() TestClusterFactory {
-	tbFactory := &defaultPersistenceTestBaseFactory{}
+	var tbFactory PersistenceTestBaseFactory = &defaultPersistenceTestBaseFactory{}
+	if CustomPersistenceTestBaseFactory != nil {
+		tbFactory = CustomPersistenceTestBaseFactory
+	}
 	return NewTestClusterFactoryWithCustomTestBaseFactory(tbFactory)
 }
 
